    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d356">

                <pb facs="0443" n="1277" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1277"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 658.119/446</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-06-15T18:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">June 15, 1918, 6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received June 16, 7.13 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2309. When the Foreign Minister handed me the note referred to in my 2308, June
                    15, 5 p.m., he also gave me the following note which I send herewith without
                    comment: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>One of the letters which were exchanged by the delegates, letter No. 7 of
                            Mr. Leverton
                                Harris to Mr. Carlsson,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d356-f1"/> contains in
                            regard to clause 3 the following passage: “It is understood, etc.”
                            Although I have been told that Mr. Carlsson suggested this
                            passage, I feel it my duty in order to avoid all misunderstanding to
                            make expressly this reserve, that the Swedish Government in adhering to
                            the arrangement did not intend in any way to recognize the right of the
                            Associated Governments to make such a requisition. I will be grateful if
                            you will kindly bring the statement to the knowledge of the Associated
                            Governments.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>A similar note at the same time was handed to the British, French, and Italian
                    Ministers to be sent to their respective Governments.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morris</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
